    "message": {
      "content": "# Distributed Tensor and Expert Parallel Inference: A Technical Note\n\n## 1. Motivation and Scope\n\nModern large models exceed the memory and compute envelope of a single accelerator. Two orthogonal parallelism strategies address this:\n\n- **Tensor Parallelism (TP)**: Splits individual weight matrices (and activations) across devices. Every device holds a shard of *every* layer and processes the *same* tokens.\n- **Expert Parallelism (EP)**: Splits *discrete* model components\u2014Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) feed-forward blocks\u2014across devices. Each device holds a subset of experts, and *different* tokens are routed to different devices.\n\nIn practice, large MoE models (Mixtral, DeepSeek-V3, Switch-style transformers) combine both: attention layers use TP, MoE layers use EP, and both sit atop data parallelism (DP) for throughput. This note covers the mechanics of TP and EP during **inference** (prefill and decode), the communication collectives involved, performance characteristics, and how to verify correctness.\n\n---\n\n## 2. Tensor Parallel Inference\n\n### 2.1 What is sharded\n\nFor a transformer layer, TP partitions along two natural dimensions:\n\n**Attention block:**\n- Query/Key/Value projection matrices `W_Q, W_K, W_V` are column-parallel: each of the `P` devices holds `d_model \u00d7 (d_head \u00b7 n_heads / P)` slices. Heads are assigned to devices so no head is split across devices.\n- Output projection `W_O` is row-parallel: each device holds `(d_head \u00b7 n_heads / P) \u00d7 d_model`.\n\n**MLP block:**\n- First GEMM `W_up`/`W_gate` (or `W_1` in GELU MLPs) is column-parallel.\n- Second GEMM `W_down` (or `W_2`) is",
      "reasoning": "",
